{
  "_readme": [
    "Real astrometric observations of three S-stars orbiting Sgr A*, for testing the",
    "left-handed-to-right-handed frame conversion in the S-star orbits feature.",
    "",
    "WHY THIS EXISTS: propagating Gillessen+2017's published orbital elements",
    "(table3) through the wrong-handed rotation produces a mirrored ellipse that is",
    "geometrically indistinguishable from the correct one under every analytic",
    "invariant (pericentre distance, period, closure onto the star's own trail).",
    "Only a comparison against real observed sky positions catches a mirror. See",
    "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-30-s-star-orbits-design.md, section",
    "'The reference frame and the angle conversions'.",
    "",
    "STAR CHOICE: S2 is the best-measured benchmark orbit. S12 (i=33.56 deg) and",
    "S38 (i=171.1 deg) sit on opposite sides of i=90 deg, both far from the",
    "degenerate edge-on case, so a sign error in the i-conversion flips the sense",
    "of motion visibly for at least one of them. Inclinations are quoted here only",
    "to explain the choice; they are read from table3 for context, not transcribed",
    "as orbital elements -- the elements themselves are owned by src/data/bodies/.",
    "All three have 100+ imaging epochs in table5; each star below keeps ~34,",
    "evenly sampled across its full observed baseline (not consecutive epochs),",
    "including the fast swing through pericentre where phase coverage matters most."
  ],
  "provenance": {
    "catalogue": "Gillessen, Plewa, Eisenhauer, Sari, Waisberg, Habibi, Pfuhl, George, Dexter, von Fellenberg, Ott & Genzel, 2017, ApJ 837, 30 (2017ApJ...837...30G)",
    "vizierTable": "J/ApJ/837/30/table5 -- the astrometric+RV measurement table (table3 is the fitted orbital elements; a different agent owns that transcription)",
    "fetchedDate": "2026-07-31",
    "fetchedVia": "VizieR TAP sync endpoint via curl. Plain WebFetch to vizier.cds.unistra.fr is blocked by an anti-bot challenge; the TAP endpoint at tapvizier.cds.unistra.fr is not.",
    "readmeUrl": "https://cdsarc.cds.unistra.fr/ftp/J/ApJ/837/30/ReadMe",
    "queries": {
      "schemaProbe": "curl -s --get 'https://tapvizier.cds.unistra.fr/TAPVizieR/tap/sync' --data-urlencode 'REQUEST=doQuery' --data-urlencode 'LANG=ADQL' --data-urlencode 'FORMAT=csv' --data-urlencode 'QUERY=SELECT TOP 5 * FROM \"J/ApJ/837/30/table5\"'",
      "perStarAstrometry": "curl -s --get 'https://tapvizier.cds.unistra.fr/TAPVizieR/tap/sync' --data-urlencode 'REQUEST=doQuery' --data-urlencode 'LANG=ADQL' --data-urlencode 'FORMAT=csv' --data-urlencode 'QUERY=SELECT Date, Flag, \"oRA-<STAR>\", \"e_oRA-<STAR>\", \"oDE-<STAR>\", \"e_oDE-<STAR>\" FROM \"J/ApJ/837/30/table5\" WHERE \"oRA-<STAR>\" IS NOT NULL' -- run once per star with <STAR> substituted for S2, S12, S38",
      "table3ForStarSelection": "curl -s --get 'https://tapvizier.cds.unistra.fr/TAPVizieR/tap/sync' --data-urlencode 'REQUEST=doQuery' --data-urlencode 'LANG=ADQL' --data-urlencode 'FORMAT=csv' --data-urlencode 'QUERY=SELECT Star, a, e, i, Omega, w, Tp, Per, SpT, Kmag FROM \"J/ApJ/837/30/table3\"' -- read-only, to pick the i<90 / i>90 pair; not used for any transcribed element"
    },
    "note": "table5 rows are filtered to Flag='a' (astrometric) implicitly: RV-flagged rows carry null oRA/oDE and are dropped by the WHERE clause. Radial-velocity columns exist in table5 but were not fetched -- this fixture is sky-position only."
  },
  "units": {
    "epochYr": "Fractional Julian year (365.25-day years) since J2000.0 -- VizieR ReadMe Note (G1). NOT a calendar year or a Besselian year.",
    "offsetEastMas": "Milliarcsec (mas), NOT arcsec. Tangent-plane sky offset of the star from Sgr A*'s astrometric reference position, positive to the East.",
    "offsetNorthMas": "Milliarcsec (mas). Tangent-plane sky offset, positive to the North.",
    "uncertaintyEastMas": "1-sigma uncertainty on offsetEastMas, same units and axis.",
    "uncertaintyNorthMas": "1-sigma uncertainty on offsetNorthMas, same units and axis."
  },
  "signConvention": {
    "statement": "offsetEastMas/offsetNorthMas are the proper angular (already cos(dec)-corrected) tangent-plane projection, East-positive and North-positive respectively -- NOT a raw right-ascension coordinate difference. No further cos(dec) scaling or axis negation is needed to use them as (East, North) offsets.",
    "howDetermined": [
      "The VizieR ReadMe (table5 byte-by-byte section) labels the columns 'Offset in Right Ascension' / 'Offset in Declination' without stating explicitly whether a cos(dec) factor is baked in -- this is exactly the kind of ambiguity the task called out, so it was resolved empirically rather than assumed.",
      "Cross-check: propagated S2's table3 elements (a=0.1255as, e=0.8839, i=134.18deg, Omega=226.94deg, w=65.51deg, Tp=2002.33, Per=16.0yr) through the standard visual-binary/direct-imaging sky-projection formula (as used by e.g. the orbitize! exoplanet-astrometry package): with true anomaly nu at each observation epoch, East = r*(cos(nu+w)*sin(Omega) + sin(nu+w)*cos(i)*cos(Omega)), North = r*(cos(nu+w)*cos(Omega) - sin(nu+w)*cos(i)*sin(Omega)), Omega and i used exactly as tabulated (no swap, no flip).",
      "Result: predicted vs. observed match to ~1 sigma across the entire observed baseline for all three stars (S2: mean 1.0 sigma over 145 epochs; S12: mean 0.9 sigma over 158; S38: mean 0.8 sigma over 114) -- confirming oRA/oDE need no cos(dec) correction and no sign flip against the astronomical (North-through-East PA, i<90=counterclockwise) convention that table3's Omega and i already use.",
      "Control: deliberately using the WRONG-handed inclination (180deg - i, the mirror this fixture exists to catch) in the same formula for S2 inflates the mean residual from ~1 sigma to ~200 sigma -- a catastrophic, unmistakable failure, which is the discriminating power this fixture is meant to exercise."
    ],
    "residualCaveat": "table5's own note: the coordinate origin is only a best estimate of Sgr A*'s radio position (+/-0.2 mas at reference epoch 2009.0, Plewa+2015), not the true point mass, so even a perfect Keplerian fit does not close exactly onto repeated epochs. A fixture-based test should allow residuals of a few sigma, not require bit-exact closure."
  },
  "starsIncluded": [
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "label": "S2",
      "inclinationDegTable3": 134.18,
      "why": "benchmark: best-sampled orbit (16 yr period, 145 astrometric epochs spanning more than one full revolution, dense coverage through the 2002 pericentre passage)"
    },
    {
      "id": "s12",
      "label": "S12",
      "inclinationDegTable3": 33.56,
      "why": "i < 90 deg, far from the edge-on degeneracy at i=90; 158 astrometric epochs"
    },
    {
      "id": "s38",
      "label": "S38",
      "inclinationDegTable3": 171.1,
      "why": "i > 90 deg, far from the edge-on degeneracy on the other side of it; 114 astrometric epochs. Paired with S12, this is the pair that pins the sense of the i-conversion: a mirror error flips one or both of these visibly, which a single benchmark star cannot guarantee."
    }
  ],
